{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"b8d63e99-1280-4b90-bbc7-16033b0cd429","step_index":122,"state":"ACTIVE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"python3 -c '\nimport re, html\nfor step in [\"36\", \"37\", \"50\", \"51\", \"62\"]:\n    p = f\"/Users/valentinakoniukhova/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/b8d63e99-1280-4b90-bbc7-16033b0cd429/.system_generated/steps/{step}/content.md\"\n    txt = open(p).read()\n    print(f\"=== STEP {step} ===\")\n    for m in re.finditer(r\"\\\"body\\\":\\\"(.*?)\\\"\", txt):\n        print( m.group(1).encode(\"utf-8\").decode(\"unicode_escape\", errors=\"ignore\") )\n    for m in re.finditer(r\"\u003cdiv class=\\x27post\\x27 itemprop=\\\"text\\\"\u003e(.*?)\u003c/div\u003e\", txt, re.S)…"}}}}
{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"b8d63e99-1280-4b90-bbc7-16033b0cd429","step_index":122,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","duration_seconds":1.6818710000000001,"tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"python3 -c '\nimport re, html\nfor step in [\"36\", \"37\", \"50\", \"51\", \"62\"]:\n    p = f\"/Users/valentinakoniukhova/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/b8d63e99-1280-4b90-bbc7-16033b0cd429/.system_generated/steps/{step}/content.md\"\n    txt = open(p).read()\n    print(f\"=== STEP {step} ===\")\n    for m in re.finditer(r\"\\\"body\\\":\\\"(.*?)\\\"\", txt):\n        print( m.group(1).encode(\"utf-8\").decode(\"unicode_escape\", errors=\"ignore\") )\n    for m in re.finditer(r\"\u003cdiv class=\\x27post\\x27 itemprop=\\\"text\\\"\u003e(.*?)\u003c/div\u003e\", txt, re.S)…"},"output":"\u003ctruncated 28 lines\u003e\n\r\n## Related\r\n\r\n- #41 â Settings permission diagnostic completed; confirms hosted runtime Settings read.\r\n- #21 â provider workout grammar audit.\r\n- #24 â source divergence for physiological anchors.\r\n- #33 â future meaningful HR ranges; not required to fix this ceiling bug.\r\n\r\n=== STEP 37 ===\r\n=== STEP 50 ===\r\n## What the provider actually documents\r\n\r\nIntervals publishes a workout-text syntax guide. Two of its rules are the exact cause of\r\nthis product's two delivery incidents:\r\n\r\n- `m` means minutes (not metres). For metres, use `mtr`.\r\n- Any text before the first duration becomes the cue text.\r\n\r\n2026-08-13 was both rules at once: a step named `éæª» 1000m` emitted\r\n`- éæª» 1000m 1km 6:05-6:20/km Pace`; `1000m` sat before the first real duration, so it\r\n*was* the duration, and it meant 1000 minutes. The provider stored 805695 m / 301440 s for\r\na 5Ã1000 m threshold session. That is documented behaviour, correctly applied. The defect\r\nwas ours.\r\n\r\nBoth fixes landed and both match the published rule â `duration_text` emits `mtr` for\r\nmetres and `km` only for whole kilometres, and `_STEP_NAME_GRAMMAR_COLLISION` refuses a step\r\nname that would occupy the duration slot. But they were derived from a live failure, not\r\nfrom the guide, and no pass has ever checked the generator against the guide as a whole.\r\n\r\n## What is not documented\r\n\r\nSending a structured document instead of the text is **not** an officially supported input.\r\nA forum thread asking exactly this (\r\n=== STEP 51 ===\r\n=== STEP 62 ===\r\nHi David,\r\nI’m building a coaching-platform integration with Intervals.icu — PROTRAIN pushes each athlete’s\r\nplanned endurance sessions into their calendar, written to `POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/bulk?upsert=true` and upserted by\r\n`external_id`. Everything looked correct on the Intervals.icu side, but the workouts were arriving\r\nempty on the athlete’s watch. I’ve isolated it to two independent problems, one of which I’ve\r\nalready solved. Byte-exact numbers below — I’d rather ask a few precise questions than a vague\r\none.\r\n## 1. The same event exports differently through the web and through the API\r\nOne of our pushed events (`Ritmo Suave`, a single 10 km step):\r\n| download path | file size | `workout_step` messages | `num_valid_steps` |\r\n|—|—|—|—|\r\n| API, `GET /events?category=WORKOUT\u0026ext=fit` | **119 bytes** | **1** | **1** |\r\n| Web UI download button | **92 bytes** | **0** | **0** |\r\nSame event, same day, no edits in between. The web download was repeated after a full re-upsert\r\nof every event, so this isn’t a stale-cache effect. `GET` on the event returns\r\n`workout_doc.steps` intact either way.\r\n## 2. A controlled A/B pair isolates the cause to the *vehicle*\r\nTwo events describing the **same** workout (10 km @ 5:30/km), differing only in how it was sent:\r\n| control | payload sent | `workout_doc` after GET | FIT via **API** | FIT via **web** |\r\n|—|—|—|—|—|\r\n| **A** | `description: “- 10km 5:30/km Pace”` only | 1 step (compiled by Intervals) | 121 b / **1 step** | 121 b / **1 step** |\r\n| **B** | `workout_doc` only, no description | 1 step (exactly as sent) | 119 b / **1 step** | 92 b / **0 steps** |\r\nA workout authored as **text** exports correctly through both paths. One authored as\r\n**`workout_doc`** exports correctly only through the API — the web generator appears not to see\r\nsteps that were uploaded as `workout_doc` rather than compiled from the description text.\r\nThis lines up with [#124215]( API: Create or update workout without using description text syntax? ),\r\nwhere a user created a workout via `workout_doc`, got well-formed steps back from the backend, and\r\nstill found “the pace range is missing” once it reached Garmin — an independent report on a real\r\ndevice rather than on a download.\r\n## 3. Pace targets need the athlete’s threshold pace — solved, but worth documenting\r\nInitially the exported step came out with no target at all:\r\n```\r\nthreshold_pace: null    →   92 bytes    step 0: duration = DISTANCE 1000000 (10 km, cm)   target = OPEN\r\n```\r\nThe pace was present in the compiled `workout_doc` the whole time —\r\n`{“pace”:{“units”:“secs/km”,“value”:330},“distance”:10000,“duration”:3300}`, and Intervals even\r\nderived the 3300 s duration from it — but it never reached the FIT.\r\nFollowing [#130706]( Pace targets lost in Garmin export for API-created running workouts — steps arrive on watch as \"No Target\" (parsed correctly in workout_doc) ),\r\nwe set a Run threshold pace in the athlete’s sport settings and re-exported the same event:\r\n```\r\nthreshold_pace set      →  146 bytes    step 0: duration = DISTANCE 1000000              target = SPEED 2.955–3.106 m/s\r\n```\r\nThat’s a 5:22–5:38 /km band around the 5:30 we prescribed, so a single prescribed value is widened\r\nby roughly ±8 s/km on export — good to know, and it confirmed for us that we should keep emitting\r\nsingle values rather than bands.\r\nSo this one is a **configuration prerequisite**, not a bug. It’s just an invisible one from an\r\nintegrator’s side: an unset threshold silently strips targets from otherwise valid workouts, and\r\nnothing in the API response hints at it. A line in the upload guide would save the next person the\r\nsame hunt.\r\n## Questions\r\n1. **Which representation feeds device sync (the Garmin push)** — the compiled one the web\r\ndownload uses, or the `workout_doc` the API download uses? Our evidence says these are two\r\ndifferent generators, and knowing which one reaches the watch decides how we should be writing.\r\n2. **Is `workout_doc` via the API a supported path, or is the contract the `description` text\r\nsyntax?** The guide in [#63624]( Uploading planned workouts to Intervals.icu )\r\ndocuments text and file uploads but never mentions `workout_doc`. If text is the intended\r\ncontract we’ll switch our emitter over — we’d just rather build against the supported path than\r\none that happens to work.\r\n3. **Is the threshold-pace prerequisite intended?** It’s surprising for an *absolute* target:\r\n`5:30/km` needs no athlete-specific reference to be expressed, yet without a threshold it’s\r\ndropped entirely rather than exported as-is.\r\nAnd one that would help regardless of the answers: **when a planned workout ends up with zero\r\nsteps, or with every target `OPEN`, is there anything we can check from the API to detect it?**\r\nRight now `GET` returns a perfectly valid `workout_doc`, so our post-write verification passes\r\nwhile the athlete’s watch is empty. That gap is what made this take a while to find.\r\nHappy to share exact payloads, decoded FIT bytes, or to run any test on our side — we have a\r\nscripted harness for it.\r\nThanks for intervals.icu, and for keeping the API open.\r\n---\r\n\r\n---\r\nMy 2 cents:\r\nThis is something we see happen more often recently. Questions that clearly show that the user has barely or ever used the Intervals platform. They are just after a hub that easily and freely transfers workouts and activities from multiple device manufacturers from and to their own, often AI, platform.\r\n---\r\nThanks R2Tom, really helpful. Fair point on the wording — I over-edited the\r\npost. We’ll switch to the description field, and the preflight idea (checking\r\nthe athlete’s zones/threshold before syncing) is exactly what we were missing.\r\nFor context: I train with Intervals myself and my athletes will be using\r\ntheir own accounts — the integration just saves my coach from typing every\r\nsession twice. Thanks again!\r\n---\r\n\r\n---\r\nThanks R2Tom — fair point on the wording, I over-edited the post.\r\nWe followed your advice and switched our emitter to the description field:\r\nsame workout that exported as 92 bytes / 0 steps now comes out as 450 bytes\r\nwith all 8 steps and proper speed targets. The preflight idea (checking the\r\nathlete’s zones before syncing) is implemented too.\r\nFor context: I train with Intervals myself and my athletes use their own\r\naccounts — the integration just saves my coach from typing every session\r\ntwice. Thanks again!\r\n---\r\n"}}}
