{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"b8d63e99-1280-4b90-bbc7-16033b0cd429","step_index":157,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","duration_seconds":1.634725,"tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"python3 -c '\nimport re\np = \"/Users/valentinakoniukhova/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/b8d63e99-1280-4b90-bbc7-16033b0cd429/.system_generated/steps/149/content.md\"\ntxt = open(p).read()\nidx = txt.find(\"\\\"rawLines\\\":\")\nif idx != -1:\n    print(txt[idx:idx+8000])\n'"},"output":"\"rawLines\":[\"# Section 11 - AI Coach Protocol\",\"\",\"**Protocol Version:** 11.75  \",\"**Last Updated:** 2026-10-16\",\"**License:** [MIT](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)\",\"\",\"### Changelog\",\"\",\"**v11.75 - Completed-session structure and report context (2026-10-16, sync.py v3.141):**\",\"- `latest.json` carries compact completed-step summaries through 28 days; `has_structure` locates detailed plan-window or fallback metrics in `intervals.json` within its 14-day retention. Plan match is separate from unchanged compliance rules. DFA is independent; cache bookkeeping stays with the full block.\",\"- Resolved fixed-power bands are supported, repeated short-recovery patterns become set rows, and plan measurements use recording time with bounded ERG-lag alignment. Current paired plans are compared without timestamp revocation. Walk/Hike structure is out of scope; other sports retain descriptive fallback.\",\"- Whole-session average power and NP never establish main-set power, including in conversation, comparison and prescription. Reports label whole-session power and show the structure branch/status. Flat planned steps now receive ordered summaries; existing repeat rendering is preserved.\",\"- Post-workout Distance includes walks. Recorded Ascent is shown for walks, runs and rides, indoors and outdoors, in display units; zero is retained and missing data omitted. Ascent is descriptive and source-dependent, never compared across barometric, GPS and virtual sources.\",\"\",\"**v11.74 - Report validity and post-workout field-list alignment (doc-only):**\",\"- The post-workout field list no longer asks for a decoupling assessment label on every session, and now lists EF. Decoupling is labelled only when the session passes the existing per-session gate under *Evidence handling* (≥ 90 min with `variability_index` ≤ 1.05 and \\u003e 0); EF is interpreted only at ≥ 20 min with the same VI limit. Otherwise the value is shown without interpretation.\",\"- The post-workout, weekly and block report templates and both project instruction files apply the same gates. The post-workout template's \\\"Sessions ≥ 1 hour\\\" decoupling rule is removed. Post-workout examples 2, 3 and 8 no longer interpret decoupling or session EF at VI 1.13–1.15.\",\"- The protocol's session field list now includes the template's existing conditional fields, both project instruction files point to the complete template field set, and VI assessment labels follow the template's steady-state-only rule.\",\"- The weekly totals lists in the protocol and both project instruction files now include Phase, weekly EF, weekly HRRc and Recovery Index, matching the post-workout template.\",\"- Gates, thresholds, aggregate durability and EF rules, and producer output are unchanged.\",\"\",\"**v11.73 - DFA drift on fixed recording thirds (`sync.py` v3.140, intervals schema 2):**\",\"- Drift now compares the first and last thirds of the original recording index, preserving gaps instead of compacting retained samples. `drift_quality` distinguishes unavailable, descriptive-only and eligible contrasts; summaries and profiles carry the status, reasons and coverage evidence. Trailing drift uses eligible sessions only and reports `drift_sessions`.\",\"- Eligibility requires verified continuous 1 Hz timing, at least 90 recorded minutes, endpoint and sixth coverage floors, bounded gaps and the existing supra-LT2 veto. Missing artifact readings are drift gaps without changing session sufficiency or observed TIZ. Non-finite alpha1 is excluded from rollups.\",\"- A physiological drift flag additionally requires delta \\u003c -0.2 and a steady easy/endurance cycling session confirmed from its structure, with fueling, heat and fatigue cross-checks. Numerical eligibility does not establish physiology; selective loss and missing hard efforts can still bias a contrast. Short sessions retain computable drift descriptively with `short_session`.\",\"- Protocol, post-workout and block-report wording follow the three states. The v11.72 failed-quality-gate refusal and the Tier-2 boundary remain unchanged.\",\"\",\"**v11.72 - DFA a1 refusal reports the failed quality gates (doc-only):**\",\"- The insufficient-data notice now identifies each failed session-quality gate: fewer than 1200 valid samples (20 nominal valid minutes at 1 Hz), valid coverage below 70%, or both. The post-workout template follows the same rule and reports an unresolved reason when the supplied quality data cannot establish it\",\"- Known artifact values above 5% are filtered before those gates; there is no separate session-average artifact or artifact-coverage sufficiency gate. Producer calculations, emitted fields and the refusal to interpret insufficient data are unchanged\",\"\",\"**v11.71 - Workout Reference Library role clarified (doc-only):**\",\"- Section 11 B §8 and the library described its role differently. §8 makes the library the binding design source: no invented session structures, gaps flagged, `session_template` recorded. `WORKOUT_REFERENCE.md` called its templates \\\"inspiration, not prescription\\\" and invited adapting, extending or replacing them, so an AI could read either behaviour into the documents\",\"- §8's opening sentence now says the AI must base a structured session on a library template and adapt its prescription within that template's permitted variants and constraints, replacing \\\"must select from\\\". The no-invention rule, gap rule, selection rules, Saved Workouts Mirror rules and audit requirements are unchanged\",\"- The library (v0.7.1) and its READMEs now state the same contract: the structure comes from the library; interval count, duration, intensity and recovery are adapted to the athlete within the template; progression only where readiness and progression rules permit. Extending or replacing templates is a human library-maintenance decision, and the AI flags gaps instead. A human-added template must be part of the library in use and carry the §1 YAML metadata\",\"\",\"**v11.70 - `history.json` catch-up, retention and a GitHub Actions refresh budget (`sync.py` v3.137):**\",\"- **A stale `history.json` could stay stale for a week or more.** `sync.py` refreshed an existing file older than 28 days only on Sunday or Monday shortly after midnight, so a delayed, skipped or sleeping run missed the window. The age rule is unchanged, but the weekday and time-of-day window is removed: outside GitHub Actions a stale file is refreshed on the first sync after it becomes due.\",\"- **A failed refresh no longer replaces good history.** When an existing file loads, a failed activities, wellness or athlete fetch keeps its data and `generated_at`, so it still shows its real age; this applies to automatic refreshes and to `--generate-history`. Only a failed automatic refresh then adds an internal `refresh_state` block that schedules retries after 1 h, 6 h and 24 h; it carries no training data. A failed `--generate-history` still fails loudly and records no retry state.\",\"- **GitHub Actions refreshes are budgeted.** A fresh runner cannot see an unpublished attempt, so in GitHub Actions an automatic refresh of an existing file starts only on runs whose workflow run number is a multiple of 16. Other runs skip only the history refresh and sync normally. A manual Sync Now skips only this limit: it still needs a due file and waits out any retry delay. The refresh can therefore come several runs later, with no fixed time limit.\",\"- First-run behavior (missing or unreadable file) is unchanged. No training-data field, coaching calculation, readiness or report rule changed. `history.json` gains only the additive internal `refresh_state` retry metadata described above, which carries no training data. Only the protocol paragraph on `history.json` refresh is updated.\",\"\",\"**v11.69 - Fictional JSON examples isolated from athlete data (doc-only):**\",\"- **The bundled examples could be mistaken for real data.** The six fictional files in `examples/json-examples/` used the same names as the generated athlete files, so a \r\n"}}}
